      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>The infiltration test has shown air leakage from the following house components:
-	Windows
-	Receptacles
-	Door
-	Fans and ventilators
During the infiltration test walkthrough, we notice air leakage on the items listed above. Airtightness is very important to achieve energy savings.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>At the time of the assessment, the insulation of the exterior walls was R-20. We recommend upgrading the insulation level. You are eligible for $2.50/square foot for the addition of R-20+, and $ 3.50/square foot for R-30+. 
	
</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>At the time of the audit, the insulation level of the attic was estimated to be R-30. We recommend adding insulation to meet the city of Whitehorse standard of R-60.</ceilings>
                <exposedFloors>Increasing the insulation of the exposed floor will contribute to reducing the heat loss from this floor area and help in energy saving. You are eligible to an incentive of $2.50/square foot for the addition of R-20 to R-29, and $ 3.50/square foot for R-30+.  </exposedFloors>
                <foundation>Most of the heat loss in a home comes from the basement. We strongly recommend adding insulation on the foundation walls and the header joist (perimeter joist). Insulating the basement will contribute directly to energy savings.  </foundation>
                <windows>We recommend the replacement of the windows by triple pane and Energy Star certified units with Lowe coating and Argon filled.</windows>
                <doors>We recommend the replacement of the wood doors with new energy star certified products.  </doors>
                <HeatingSystem>Have you already thought about having a heat pump? This type of heating system can use up to 3 times less energy than a typical baseboard and needs electricity to operate. You might be eligible to participate in the Heat Pumps pilot project, please follow the link below for more information;
https://yukon.ca/en/news/expanded-rebates-renewable-home-heating-now-available

</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables>For any questions concerning the rebates and the programs about solar energy, please follow the link below to get more information:
https://yukon.ca/en/renewable-energy-rebate#eligible-renewable-systems


